==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|attercoppe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ātorcoppe|t=spider}}, corresponding to {{compound|en|atter|gloss1=poison, venom|cop|gloss2=spider}}. The latter is still to be found in the English word {{m|en|cobweb}}. Cognate to {{cog|nl|etterkop||peevish or ill-natured person}} and {{cog|da|edderkop||spider}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈætəkɒp/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-attercop.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|dialectal|Northern England}} A [[spider]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1924|author=w:Robert Graves|title=Attercop: the All-Wise Spider
|passage=Myself, not bound by James’ view<br>Nor Walter’s, in a vision saw these two<br>Like trapped and weakening flies<br>In toils of the same hoary net;<br>I seemed to hear ancestral cries<br>Buzzing ‘To our All-Wise, Omnivorous<br>'''Attercop''' glowering over us,<br>Whose table we have set<br>With blood and bones and sweat.’}}
#* {{RQ:Tolkien Hobbit|page=147|passage=Old fat spider spinning in a tree!<br>Old fat spider can’t see me!<br>'''Attercop'''! '''Attercop'''!<br>Won’t you stop,<br>Stop your spinning and look for me?}}
# {{lb|en|dialectal|Northern England}} A [[peevish]] or [[ill-natured]] person.

